<details><summary>Click to reveal the telescoping calculation</summary>
With $\eta_k=1/(k+2)$, each transition multiplies the state by $(k+1)/(k+2)$. The product telescopes to $1/(k+1)$; quadratic energy therefore scales by $1/(k+1)^2$.
</details>

## Direction is not destination

Natural gradient selects an intrinsic tangent vector, but coordinate Euler addition need not follow the exact geodesic when the metric varies.

